.\" Generated from mcdiff6.md by maint/update-man-in.sh. Do not edit.
.TH MCDIFF6 1 "September 2026" "MCommander {{MAN_VERSION}}" "M\-Commander"

.SH NAME
.PP
mcdiff6 \- Internal diff viewer of M\-Commander.


.SH USAGE
.PP
\fBmcdiff6\fP
[\-bcdfhstVx?] file1 file2

.PP
\fBmcdiff6\fP
[\-bcdfhstVx?] [\-o output] local base remote

.PP
\fBmcdiff6\fP
[\-bcdfhstVx?] [\-o output] \-B base local remote


.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
mcdiff6 is a link to
\fBmcommander\fP,
the main M\-Commander executable.  Executing
M\-Commander under this name requests starting the internal diff viewer
which compares
\fIfile1\fP
and
\fIfile2\fP
specified on the command line. Given three files, it merges
\fIlocal\fP
and
\fIremote\fP,
two changed versions of
\fIbase\fP;
see
Merge of three files\&.


.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fI\-b\fP
Force black and white display.

.TP
\fI\-c\fP
Force color mode on terminals where
\fBmcdiff6\fP
defaults to black and white.

.TP
\fI\-d\fP
Disable mouse support.

.TP
\fI\-h, \-?, \-\-help\fP
Show the options and what they do.

.TP
\fI\-B file, \-\-base=file\fP
The common base of the two files: open their merge of three files, as with
the base given between them.

.TP
\fI\-o file, \-\-output=file\fP
Where the merge of three files is saved. Without it, the merge is saved
into the base file.

.TP
\fI\-f\fP
Display the compiled\-in search paths for M\-Commander files.

.TP
\fI\-S arg, \-\-skin=arg\fP
Specify a name of skin in the command line.  See the
\fBSkins\fP
section in mcommander(1) for more information.

.TP
\fI\-s\fP
Run on a slow terminal: the screen is drawn with fewer updates.

.TP
\fI\-t\fP
Used only if the code was compiled with S\-Lang and terminfo: it makes
the M\-Commander use the value of the
\fBTERMCAP\fP
variable for the terminal information instead of the information on
the system wide terminal database

.TP
\fI\-V\fP
Displays the version of the program.

.TP
\fI\-x\fP
Forces xterm mode.  Used when running on xterm\-capable terminals (two
screen modes, and able to send mouse escape sequences).


.SH Internal Diff Viewer
.PP
The mcdiff6 is a visual diff tool. You can compare two files and edit them
in place; the difference is computed anew after every change. The git panel
plugin opens it as well, with the file as HEAD has it on one side and the
file of the working tree on the other.

.PP
Following shortcuts are available in internal diff viewer of
M\-Commander.

.TP
\fBF1\fP
Invoke the built\-in hypertext help viewer.

.TP
\fBF2\fP
Save modified files.

.TP
\fBTab\fP
Switch the active file. Its frame is double; the frame of the other file is
single and gray. A mark on the frame of the active file shows the top line,
the line F5 and F15 act on.

.TP
\fBF4\fP
Edit the active file in the internal editor.

.TP
\fBF14\fP
Edit the other file in the internal editor.

.TP
\fBF5\fP
Copy the current hunk from the active file into the other one. The current
hunk is the one at the top line. Only the current hunk is copied, and the
difference is computed again. The key bar shows the direction: "Copy\->" or
"<\-Copy".

.TP
\fBF15\fP
Copy the current hunk the other way, from the other file into the active one.

.TP
\fBShift\-F6\fP
Merge the two files against their common base: the view asks for the base
file and shows the merge of three files. F2 there asks first where to save
the result.

.TP
\fBF7\fP
Start search.

.TP
\fBF17\fP
Continue search.

.TP
\fBF9\fP
Open the
options of the compare view\&.

.TP
\fBAlt\-e\fP
Choose the charset the two files are read in.

.TP
\fBF10, Esc, q, Q\fP
Exit from diff viewer.

.TP
\fBAlt\-s, s\fP
Toggle show of hunk status.

.TP
\fBAlt\-n, l\fP
Toggle show of line numbers.

.TP
\fBCtrl\-s\fP
Toggle syntax highlighting. The text of each line is then colored by the
syntax rules, the way the internal editor colors it, and the state of the line
is left to the background and to the marker column. Where a skin tells a
changed word from the rest of its line by the color of the text alone, the word
is underlined instead. The setting is remembered separately from the editor's
own.

.TP
\fBf\fP
Show only the active file, on the whole screen.

.TP
\fB=\fP
Make panels equal in width.

.TP
\fB>\fP
Reduce the size of the right panel.

.TP
\fB<\fP
Reduce the size of the left panel.

.TP
\fB2, 3, 4, 8\fP
Set tabulation size

.TP
\fBC\-u\fP
Swap contents of diff panels.

.TP
\fBC\-r\fP
Read both files again and compute the difference anew.

.TP
\fBC\-o\fP
Toggle the terminal and show the command screen.

.TP
\fBEnter, Space, n\fP
Find next diff hunk.

.TP
\fBBackspace, p\fP
Find previous diff hunk.

.TP
\fBg, G\fP
Go to line.

.TP
\fBDown\fP
Scroll one line forward.

.TP
\fBUp\fP
Scroll one line backward.

.TP
\fBPageUp\fP
Move one page up.

.TP
\fBPageDown\fP
Moves one page down.

.TP
\fBLeft, Right\fP
Scroll the text one column sideways.

.TP
\fBC\-Left, C\-Right\fP
Scroll the text eight columns sideways.

.TP
\fBHome\fP
Scroll back to the first column.

.TP
\fBC\-Home\fP
Move to the file beginning.

.TP
\fBC\-End\fP
Move to the file end.


.SH Merge of three files
.PP
Started with three files, the compare view shows three columns: the local
file on the left, the result of the merge in the middle, and the remote file
on the right. The two side files are never changed; only the middle column is
saved.

.PP
At the start the middle column is the base file with every change of one
side already taken in. Where both sides changed the same lines, each in its
own way, the middle keeps the lines of the base: this is a conflict, shown in
the conflict color and counted after "Cnf" on the status line of the middle
column. Lines that both sides changed the same way are taken as well.

.PP
The top line of the frame of the middle column names the file the result
is saved into, after "Result:"; its bottom line names the base, after "Base:".

.PP
Every difference is computed again after each change of the middle column.
A screen narrower than 120 columns shows the middle column and one side.

.TP
\fBF5\fP
Add the lines of the left file to the current chunk of the middle column,
after what it has. The current chunk is the one at the top line, or the
first one on the screen.

.TP
\fBShift\-F5\fP
Take the lines of the left file out of the chunk again. With no side left,
the chunk has the lines of the base: a conflict is a conflict again.

.TP
\fBF6\fP
Add the lines of the right file to the chunk. With both sides added, the
lines come in the order they were added.

.TP
\fBShift\-F6\fP
Take the lines of the right file out again.

.TP
\fB], [\fP
Go to the next or previous conflict.

.TP
\fBEnter, Space, n; Backspace, p\fP
Go to the next or previous chunk.

.TP
\fBF4\fP
Edit the middle column in the editor. A chunk edited there counts as
resolved.

.TP
\fBF2\fP
Save the middle column. When conflicts are left, the view asks first.

.TP
\fBC\-u\fP
On a narrow screen, show the other side.

.TP
\fBF10\fP
Quit. When the middle column was changed and not saved, the view asks
whether to save it. Leaving without a change keeps the files as they are.

.PP
The program ends with the exit status 0 when the merge was saved with no
conflict left, and 1 otherwise, so it can serve as the merge tool of git:

.EX
[merge]
    tool = mcdiff
[mergetool "mcdiff"]
    cmd = mcdiff6 "$LOCAL" "$BASE" "$REMOTE" \-o "$MERGED"
    trustExitCode = true

.EE


.SH Diff options
.PP
The settings of the
compare view,
as
\fBF9\fP
there and the
\fBDiff viewer options\fP
entry of the Options menu of the file manager open them. The compare view
takes them when it starts, so a view already on the screen keeps the ones it
was opened with.

.PP
\fIDiff algorithm.\fP
Normal compares the files as they are. Fastest uses the histogram
algorithm, which stays fast when the files differ a lot. Minimal spends more
time to find a smaller set of changes.

.PP
\fIIgnore case.\fP
Upper and lower case letters count as the same character.

.PP
\fIIgnore tab expansion.\fP
Lines that differ only in how the same indentation is written, with tabs or
with spaces, count as equal.

.PP
\fIIgnore space change.\fP
A run of whitespace counts as equal to any other run of whitespace.

.PP
\fIIgnore all whitespace.\fP
Whitespace is left out of the comparison.

.PP
\fIStrip trailing carriage return.\fP
Drop the carriage return at the end of a line, so a file with DOS line ends
compares to one with Unix line ends.


.SH FILES
.TP
\fI{{pkgdatadir}}/help/mcommander.md\fP
The help file for the program.

.TP
\fI{{pkgdatadir}}/mc.ini\fP
The default system\-wide setup for M\-Commander, used only if
the user's own ~/.config/mc6/ini file is missing.

.TP
\fI{{pkgdatadir}}/defaults.ini\fP
Global settings for the M\-Commander.  Settings in this file
affect all users, whether they have ~/.config/mc6/ini or not.

.TP
\fI~/.config/mc6/ini\fP
User's own setup.  If this file is present, the setup is loaded from
here instead of the system\-wide startup file.


